unsolved.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Black holes</span><span class=\"tag\">Gravitational waves</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The UFO boom is not an astronomy question, it is a question of belief When the evidence stops at testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The further science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life looks in the observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we are really talking about is ourselves. EconTalk EconTalk Are We Alone? Cosmic Loneliness and the Longing to Believe (with Adam Kirsch) UFOs Extraterrestrial life Philosophy of science Anti-expert sentiment Social psychology UAP euphemism treadmill book experience Drake equation AATIP\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-econtalk-所有外星人想象都是人类的自画像/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:11:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The UFO boom is not an astronomy question, it is a question of belief</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the evidence stops at testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The further science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life looks in the observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we are really talking about is ourselves.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">UFOs</span><span class=\"tag\">Extraterrestrial life</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants The US sees about 130,000 new cases of kidney failure a year and performs fewer than 30,000 transplants. Al Roth takes apart where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploitation. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area Kidneys for sale Kidney Transplants Organ Sales Market Design Moral Controversy Public Policy repugnant transaction market design non-directed donor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-grayarea-禁止卖肾的代价-本不该失去的生命/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7c8b1deddea1e240-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">49:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The US sees about 130,000 new cases of kidney failure a year and performs fewer than 30,000 transplants. Al Roth takes apart where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploitation.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Kidney Transplants</span><span class=\"tag\">Organ Sales</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — your brain has priced the effort too high Neurologist Masud Husain uses case histories and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy come from a perceived effort cost that is too high, not from an absence of desire. Lowering the activation energy, planning ahead and breaking tasks into steps are the practical ways around the barrier. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab How to Improve Motivation &amp; Overcome Procrastination | Dr. Masud Husain Motivation Procrastination Dopamine Neuroscience Alzheimer&#x27;s disease Addiction apathy basal ganglia activation energy wanting vs liking\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-huberman-动机不是欲望问题-而是激活能量障碍/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e03163dcfb779e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">2:20:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — your brain has priced the effort too high</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Neurologist Masud Husain uses case histories and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy come from a perceived effort cost that is too high, not from an absence of desire. Lowering the activation energy, planning ahead and breaking tasks into steps are the practical ways around the barrier.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Motivation</span><span class=\"tag\">Procrastination</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Africa isn&#x27;t short of resources. It&#x27;s short of population density Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession, then spent $20 billion of his own money to build Nigeria&#x27;s refinery and become Africa&#x27;s richest man. But Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can repeat the Asian miracle is the urban market size that comes with population density, not minerals and not aid. Odd Lots Odd Lots The Nigerian Industrial Behemoth That Could Reshape the African Economy African economy industrial policy manufacturing demographics China-Africa relations land reform flying geese paradigm resource curse developmental state kulak demonstration effect\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-oddlots-非洲工业化不缺资源-缺的是人口密度/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">58:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Africa isn&#x27;t short of resources. It&#x27;s short of population density</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession, then spent $20 billion of his own money to build Nigeria&#x27;s refinery and become Africa&#x27;s richest man. But Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can repeat the Asian miracle is the urban market size that comes with population density, not minerals and not aid.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">African economy</span><span class=\"tag\">industrial policy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Water privatisation is not destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s referendum on the right to water On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans voted 64.7% in favour of writing ‘water is a natural resource essential to life’ into the constitution, and of restricting water supply to the state alone — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out at constitutional level. The movement began with a tap being shut off in a coastal city. Witness History Witness History Uruguay&#x27;s water referendum Water rights Referendum Privatisation Uruguay Social movements Constitution concession mate\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-witness-供水私有化不是宿命-乌拉圭公投的水权胜利/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Water privatisation is not destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s referendum on the right to water</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans voted 64.7% in favour of writing ‘water is a natural resource essential to life’ into the constitution, and of restricting water supply to the state alone — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out at constitutional level. The movement began with a tap being shut off in a coastal city.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Water rights</span><span class=\"tag\">Referendum</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Only 60 of the 5,000 People on a Carrier Fight; the Rest Restock Vending Machines On a U.S. carrier after 9/11, only fifty or sixty of the 5,000 people aboard ever touch a bomb. Everyone else spends the war restocking vending machines, doing laundry, and passing rumors — while the captain&#x27;s biggest worry is whether his sailors are watching the news. This American Life This American Life 206: Somewhere in the Arabian Sea U.S. military aircraft carrier daily life at war organizational management portraits of the young 9/11 blood chit Tailhook scandal Hollywood shower berthing XO / executive officer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-tal-航母战争的日常-补零食-防谈恋爱-等回家/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1da611bfe52eccd0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">This American Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Only 60 of the 5,000 People on a Carrier Fight; the Rest Restock Vending Machines</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On a U.S. carrier after 9/11, only fifty or sixty of the 5,000 people aboard ever touch a bomb. Everyone else spends the war restocking vending machines, doing laundry, and passing rumors — while the captain&#x27;s biggest worry is whether his sailors are watching the news.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">U.S. military</span><span class=\"tag\">aircraft carrier</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Screens train multitasking, not learning: the harder classrooms digitize, the worse kids do The harder classrooms have pushed digital tools since 2010, the further children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have fallen across the board; screens train multitasking rather than learning, and AI only makes the errors harder to see. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Tech Making Our Kids Less Capable? With Dr Jared Cooney Horvath Ed tech Classroom screens Cognitive development AI in education Digital literacy Contrarian Ed tech ICILS Offloading Outsourcing SRLs Drill-and-kill\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-iq2-技术不等于学习-屏幕正在让一代人变笨/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/46f8e9d9c37f6c72-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">33:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Screens train multitasking, not learning: the harder classrooms digitize, the worse kids do</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The harder classrooms have pushed digital tools since 2010, the further children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have fallen across the board; screens train multitasking rather than learning, and AI only makes the errors harder to see.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ed tech</span><span class=\"tag\">Classroom screens</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"British misjudgement lit the fuse in Northern Ireland: the Provisional IRA meant to fight all along A run of British government misjudgements in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, the refusal to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — turned the Provisional IRA from a fringe armed group into the central actor of a thirty-year conflict, and radicalised British soldiers along the way. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 699. The Troubles: Return of the IRA (Part 3) The Troubles IRA Counter-insurgency British politics Insurgency Sectarian violence The Troubles Operation Demetrius UVF\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-restishistory-临时ira绝非防御组织-英军误判铸成三十年冲突/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:51</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>British misjudgement lit the fuse in Northern Ireland: the Provisional IRA meant to fight all along</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A run of British government misjudgements in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, the refusal to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — turned the Provisional IRA from a fringe armed group into the central actor of a thirty-year conflict, and radicalised British soldiers along the way.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">IRA</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Mental events follow no laws, yet reasons can still be causes that leave us free Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are identical to physical events only as particulars; but in the explanation of action, reasons are genuinely causes, and when a reason causes an action the agent is precisely then free. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 398: Donald Davidson on Mind vs. Body (Part Two) Mind-body problem Philosophy of action Anomalous monism Causal explanation Davidson Analytic philosophy token identity homonomic generalization heteronomic generalization pro attitude primary reason rationalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-pel-心物无规律-理由仍可作为原因而自由/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4024c3bbaf655b13-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">53:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Mental events follow no laws, yet reasons can still be causes that leave us free</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are identical to physical events only as particulars; but in the explanation of action, reasons are genuinely causes, and when a reason causes an action the agent is precisely then free.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Mind-body problem</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy of action</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"A 22-Year-Old CEO, Five Rounds, Over $100 Million: The First Term Sheet Decides Everything In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi has grown RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds totalling over $100 million, and RPO shipping around 100 units a month. His calls: the first round&#x27;s terms decide whether you live or die, open source is a moat because it lowers the friction of working with you, and the robot body is the entry point to embodied AI. 十字路口Crossing 十字路口Crossing 22 岁的具身 CEO、5 轮融资、过亿美元、“不知天有多高”、“一年吃了十年的苦”｜对谈黄一：萝博派对创始人/CEO Embodied AI Robotics Fundraising Open Source Startup Management Org Design missing layer deep pocket BFM (behavior foundation model) sim2real honeycomb structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-crossing-具身创业最优解-快融资-软条款-全栈开源/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3434775ccb19b8d7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"十\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">十字路口Crossing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>A 22-Year-Old CEO, Five Rounds, Over $100 Million: The First Term Sheet Decides Everything</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi has grown RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds totalling over $100 million, and RPO shipping around 100 units a month. His calls: the first round&#x27;s terms decide whether you live or die, open source is a moat because it lowers the friction of working with you, and the robot body is the entry point to embodied AI.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Robotics Fundraising</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"What Etched&#x27;s chip really solves may be bandwidth, not compute Etched welds the transformer into hardware with 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors and direct cross-chip SRAM access, but the host suspects the problem actually being solved is bandwidth, not compute. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato The Reason My Phone is Ringing Daily: Etched AI chips Transformer-specific chips Compute efficiency Funding Bandwidth bottleneck tape out RDMA threshold voltage cluster scale memory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-techtechpotato-etched的芯片-可能解决的是带宽而不是算力/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5ab75e84ff855e23-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>What Etched&#x27;s chip really solves may be bandwidth, not compute</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Etched welds the transformer into hardware with 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors and direct cross-chip SRAM access, but the host suspects the problem actually being solved is bandwidth, not compute.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI chips</span><span class=\"tag\">Transformer-specific chips</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The moat in evals is not the AI — it is human taste You cannot leave eval design to the AI: it is good at top-down criteria, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled out of error data — have to come from a human, and only then get executed at scale with Claude Code. Peter Yang Peter Yang How to Build Better AI Evals with Claude Code in 5 Steps | Shreya &amp; Hamel Eval systems Claude Code Error analysis LLM products Human review bottom-up evals top-down evals LLM judge error discovery staccato fragments slop\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-peteryang-自下而上的评估-是-ai-给不了你的护城河/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/49510d80b4317525-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">54:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Peter Yang</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>The moat in evals is not the AI — it is human taste</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">You cannot leave eval design to the AI: it is good at top-down criteria, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled out of error data — have to come from a human, and only then get executed at scale with Claude Code.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Eval systems</span><span class=\"tag\">Claude Code</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ebola infects neurons and keeps shedding virus for 120 days; a fish cancer spreads between fish Brain organoids show that Ebola can infect neurons and keep releasing infectious virus for 120 days; melanomas in a brown bullhead population descend from a single clonal ancestor and spread from fish to fish. The boundaries of viral persistence and of transmissible cancer get rewritten at the same time. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1351: Ebola Virus Brains and Contagious Catfish Cancers Viral persistence Brain organoids Ebola Transmissible cancer NIH funding Biosafety productive persistence nonproductive persistence BSL-4 MOI\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-twiv-埃博拉持续感染脑神经元-鱼类黑色素瘤能传染/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">1:41:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Ebola infects neurons and keeps shedding virus for 120 days; a fish cancer spreads between fish</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Brain organoids show that Ebola can infect neurons and keep releasing infectious virus for 120 days; melanomas in a brown bullhead population descend from a single clonal ancestor and spread from fish to fish. The boundaries of viral persistence and of transmissible cancer get rewritten at the same time.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Viral persistence</span><span class=\"tag\">Brain organoids</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Terror birds weren&#x27;t killed by humans — the Isthmus of Panama closed first Terror birds were the apex avian predators that emerged on the island continent of South America after the dinosaurs died out, and they ruled for tens of millions of years; their eventual disappearance is tied to the great species interchange that followed the joining of the Isthmus of Panama, earlier than the arrival of humans in South America. The Ancients The Ancients Terror Birds Paleontology Bird evolution South America Extinction Asteroid impact Seriemas Eocene Isthmus of Panama\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-ancients-恐怖鸟死于物种大交换而非人类/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/270440780adbe49b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">45:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Terror birds weren&#x27;t killed by humans — the Isthmus of Panama closed first</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Terror birds were the apex avian predators that emerged on the island continent of South America after the dinosaurs died out, and they ruled for tens of millions of years; their eventual disappearance is tied to the great species interchange that followed the joining of the Isthmus of Panama, earlier than the arrival of humans in South America.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Paleontology</span><span class=\"tag\">Bird evolution</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Ukrainians Are Fighting the War With Poems and Artifacts, Not Weapons How the war turned a culture reporter into a language student and prose writers into poets: Ukrainians are resisting the war&#x27;s erasure of identity and memory by rescuing artifacts and writing poems. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Ukraine’s Culture Worth Fighting For? With Charlotte Higgins Ukraine Culture and war Poetry Museums Identity War narrative Cyrillic alphabet Lviv Book Forum citizen army\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-iq2-在乌克兰-文化是值得用生命去救的东西/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/0eefaf93ef4770ba-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">37:37</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Ukrainians Are Fighting the War With Poems and Artifacts, Not Weapons</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">How the war turned a culture reporter into a language student and prose writers into poets: Ukrainians are resisting the war&#x27;s erasure of identity and memory by rescuing artifacts and writing poems.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ukraine</span><span class=\"tag\">Culture and war</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Encrypted Reasoning Is Not a Secret: A Small Model Will Read the Big One&#x27;s Thinking Out Loud Frontier models hand the user an encrypted copy of their thinking along with the answer, but that ciphertext can be replayed verbatim into a smaller model from the same family, which will simply read it out — opening the door to privacy leaks, jailbreaks, prompt injection, and even a channel for distilling capability. Machine Learning Street Talk ML Street Talk Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs — Ilia Shumailov &amp; Alexander Panfilov Reasoning encryption Jailbreaks Model distillation AI security Prompt injection Privacy leaks reasoning trace prefill replay attack responsible disclosure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-mlst-加密思考不是秘密-小模型能把大模型的推理念出来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7306f7dff4dc8ceb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">49:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Machine Learning Street Talk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Encrypted Reasoning Is Not a Secret: A Small Model Will Read the Big One&#x27;s Thinking Out Loud</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Frontier models hand the user an encrypted copy of their thinking along with the answer, but that ciphertext can be replayed verbatim into a smaller model from the same family, which will simply read it out — opening the door to privacy leaks, jailbreaks, prompt injection, and even a channel for distilling capability.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Reasoning encryption</span><span class=\"tag\">Jailbreaks</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The people who find AI incidents are never the safety evaluators In every agent-gone-wrong incident this summer, the ones who noticed first were infrastructure security teams — researchers running evals account for precisely zero cases; and to keep pace with attacks, defenders are being forced to hand more permissions to the very agents just shown to cross the line. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution AI in the AM — Weekly Highlights: Relaunch Week (Aug 17–20, 2026) safety evals agent incidents inference cost open weights compute AI governance CoBench mind virus ghostjacking effective compute consumption Minimal Standard for Safeguards\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-cogrev-ai-事故的发现者从来不是安全评估员/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dd01d2cd4bc88e5a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:33:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>The people who find AI incidents are never the safety evaluators</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In every agent-gone-wrong incident this summer, the ones who noticed first were infrastructure security teams — researchers running evals account for precisely zero cases; and to keep pace with attacks, defenders are being forced to hand more permissions to the very agents just shown to cross the line.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">safety evals</span><span class=\"tag\">agent incidents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Food Safety Standards Born of Space Food: Testing the Finished Product Is Never Enough HACCP, the core of the global food safety system, was born out of NASA&#x27;s space food program: testing the finished product is never enough, and the whole process from ingredient to person has to be controlled; a 1971 botulism case and the 1993 Jack in the Box outbreak turned it into a mandatory global standard. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class SYMHC Classics: Astronaut Origins of Food Safety Food Safety HACCP NASA Regulation Public Health Food Industry HACCP / Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points CCP / Critical Control Point Codex Alimentarius FMEA / Failure Mode and Effects Analysis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-stuffmissed-haccp-为防止宇航员食物中毒而生的全球食安标准/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Food Safety Standards Born of Space Food: Testing the Finished Product Is Never Enough</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">HACCP, the core of the global food safety system, was born out of NASA&#x27;s space food program: testing the finished product is never enough, and the whole process from ingredient to person has to be controlled; a 1971 botulism case and the 1993 Jack in the Box outbreak turned it into a mandatory global standard.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Food Safety</span><span class=\"tag\">HACCP</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"A question is defined by the act of seeking information, not the question mark A real question is defined by its function, not its form; asking is a power you can practise — and most of the answers AI hands out today have not answered any question that was seriously posed. Philosophy Bites Philosophy Bites Lani Watson on the Power of Questions Questioning Philosophy Education AI criticism Communication Socrates information seeking act rhetorical question private questions and social questions answer oriented model\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-phibites-真正的问题不是问号-而是寻求信息的行动/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/243c92b44ec40dbb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">16:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Philosophy Bites</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>A question is defined by the act of seeking information, not the question mark</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A real question is defined by its function, not its form; asking is a power you can practise — and most of the answers AI hands out today have not answered any question that was seriously posed.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Questioning</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy</span>\n</div></div></a>"]